# Holiday gigs (playtest 2, M6; X15): working on campus over a break, you take one of two gigs open to anyone
# (the grounds, the kitchens), or, at a term job's top rank, its holiday version (`gig_library`, `gig_newt`,
# `gig_pell`, `gig_post`), which pays a little better. A gig pays through its cards; board is still paid.
# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.

@event holiday_gigs
title: Holiday Work
trigger: queued
type: menu
class: story
tone: warm
art: coin
tags: job money
scene: A noticeboard by the castle gates in the emptying holidays, two handwritten notices pinned side by side.
text: Two notices are still pinned by the gates. {season == "winter": The snow has got under one of them.|The sun has curled the corners of both.} Old Maud wants hands for the grounds. The Refectory kitchens want someone who can peel fast and not complain about it. {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: A third notice has your name on it.|}
> grounds: Work the grounds for Old Maud
  hint: Outdoor work, heavy and honest. Good pay; hard on the body.
  gain gig_grounds
  say: Maud looks you up and down, hands you a pair of gloves two sizes too big, and says, "You'll grow into them."
> kitchens: Work the Refectory kitchens
  hint: Long hours by the ovens. Good pay; hard on the temper.
  gain gig_kitchens
  say: The head cook gives you an apron, a knife and a sack of onions, and doesn't ask your name until the sack is empty.
> library: Run the library's holiday stocktake
  when: rank(library_assistant) >= 3
  hint: For senior clerks. Better pay; long days in the stacks.
  gain gig_library
  say: The head librarian hands you the stocktake ledger, a lamp and a pair of gauntlets. "Count everything," she says. "Twice, if it moves."
> newt: Run the Newt through the holiday rush
  when: rank(tavern) >= 3
  hint: For the Newt's managers. The best tips of the year; the longest nights.
  gain gig_newt
  say: The landlady gives you the keys and goes to visit her sister. "Don't let the fire go out," she says, "or the village."
> pell: Mix the season's remedies at Pell's
  when: rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3
  hint: For assistant apothecaries. Good pay; the village depends on you.
  gain gig_pell
  say: Old Pell ties an apron on you himself. "Half the valley will be coughing by next week," he says, delighted. "We'll be rich."
> post: Fly the holiday post
  when: rank(broom_courier) >= 3
  hint: For chief couriers. Every parcel in the valley; the weather against you.
  gain gig_post
  say: The loft is stacked to the rafters with parcels in brown paper. Your name is chalked on the board above the busiest route.

# The notices go up in a term's last month, so a player can plan the break (X15).
@event holiday_notices
title: Notices by the Gate
trigger: auto
slot: afternoon
when: term and (month == 4 or month == 10) and not (final_year and month == 10)
repeat: 6
priority: 30
class: routine
tone: light
art: paper
tags: job money
scene: A noticeboard by the castle gates near the end of term, fresh notices for holiday work pinned over the old ones, students stopping to read.
text: {Holiday work notices are going up by the gates, for the grounds and the kitchens, both paying better than any term job.|The holiday notices again, the same two, in fresher ink.|The notices by the gate. You know them by heart.} {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: One is addressed to you by name, from your own job.|{Someone has written "and board!" under one.|A first-year is copying them out.|The wind is trying to take them.}}
> left: Pin them up for the cook
  gold +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {The head cook pays you a coin to pin the new notices straight. Working the break would mean no lie-ins, and real money.|You pin them up, get a coin for it, and read the hours. It's tempting, and tiring just to read.|You pin them up against the wind for the head cook, who pays you a coin and asks, again, whether you'll be staying.}
> right: Walk past
  mood +tiny
  say: {The break is for sleeping, you decide, and walk on. The notices flap behind you.|Not this time. You'll choose when the break comes.|You walk past. You know them by heart, and the break is still for sleeping.}

# ---------------- The grounds (gig_grounds) ----------------

@event gig_grounds_hard_ground
title: Hard Ground
plan: work
when: has(gig_grounds)
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 30
art: leaf
tags: job nature money
scene: A student and an old woman working a frozen garden bed at dawn, breath steaming, a wheelbarrow between them.
text: {season == "winter": The ground is frozen two fingers deep and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.|The ground is baked hard and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.} "The soil doesn't take holidays," she says. {~She hands you the heavier spade.|The bees watch you from their hives with professional interest.|She is already on her third bed.}
> left: Dig until it's done
  gold +large
  health -small
  vigor +small
  say: {~By noon your hands have blisters on their blisters. Maud counts your wages into them anyway.|You turn every bed. Maud inspects them, says "Hm," and pays you a little over.|You dig until Maud's third bed is matched by your third. She pays you, and gives you the lighter spade next time.}
> right: Work at Maud's pace
  gold +medium
  health -tiny
  say: {~Maud works slowly and never stops, and by evening you understand why. Less done, a smaller purse, an easier back.|You keep to her pace and her stories. She pays for the beds you turned, not the ones she did.|You work at her pace, and she still finishes three beds to your one. She pays for yours, and laughs about it.}

@event gig_grounds_storm_damage
title: After the Storm
plan: work
when: has(gig_grounds)
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 30
art: tree
tags: job nature money
scene: A fallen branch across an orchard path after a storm, a student with a saw, an old woman pointing at something higher up.
text: {season == "winter": Last night's storm brought down half a pear tree.|A summer storm in the night has stripped the orchard.} Maud wants it cleared before the stay-behinds go walking. A cracked limb still hangs over the path.
> left: Climb up and cut it down
  gold +large
  challenge feat apprentice+:
    win:
      say: {~The limb comes down where you meant it to. Maud pays you extra for not falling.|You lower it on a rope like a professional. Maud pretends not to be impressed.}
    lose:
      health -medium
      say: {~The limb comes down where it wanted to. So do you, more or less. Maud pays you and sends you to the infirmary.|You get the limb and the limb gets you.}
> right: Rope it off and clear the rest
  gold +medium
  say: {~You clear the path and rope off the danger. Maud nods: she'll ask someone with a ladder. The pay is for the path.|Safe and sensible, and a smaller purse for it.}

@event gig_grounds_hive_check
title: Checking the Hives
plan: work
when: has(gig_grounds)
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 30
art: leaf
tags: job nature
scene: A student in a veiled hat leaning over an open beehive beside an old woman, who isn't wearing one.
text: Maud's hives sit at the end of the orchard and she checks them {season == "winter": by ear in winter, knocking and listening|by hand in summer, slow and bare-armed}. Today she wants you to do it. "Tell them who you are," she says. "They like manners."
> left: Introduce yourself properly
  gold +medium
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {~You tell the bees your name and your house. Only one stings you, on principle. Maud pays you and calls you a natural.|You feel foolish for about a minute, and then you don't. The hive hums back, and one bee makes a point of it.}
> right: Get it done quickly
  gold +large
  chance even:
    say: {~Quick and quiet. The bees let it pass this once.|You're done before the bees notice. Maud notices.}
  else:
    health -small
    say: {~The bees take your hurry personally. Maud pays you and pulls out the stings with no sympathy at all.|Three stings and a lesson about manners.}

# ---------------- The Refectory kitchens (gig_kitchens) ----------------

@event gig_kitchens_onions
title: The Onion Sack
plan: work
when: has(gig_kitchens)
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 30
art: meal
tags: job money
scene: A student at a scrubbed kitchen table behind a mountain of onions, eyes streaming, a huge cook stirring a cauldron behind them.
text: {The holiday kitchens feed fewer mouths and work no fewer hours.|Back at the long table, back to the sack.|The head cook has started saving the worst sack for you, as a compliment.} {season == "winter": The Midwinter stock needs a hundred onions.|The summer stew needs a hundred onions.} Nobody has ever needed fewer.
> left: Peel the whole sack
  gold +large
  mood -small
  say: {A hundred onions and not one tear left in you. The head cook pays you and adds a heel of fresh bread.|You finish the sack. The head cook counts the onions, then your wages, both correctly.|You peel the worst sack in the kitchen and don't cry once. The head cook calls it a personal best, and pays it.}
> right: Peel, then help at the stove
  gold +medium
  alchemy +tiny
  say: {Half a sack and an hour at the stove. Less pay, but now you know why the soup tastes like home.|The head cook lets you stir. Somehow the stew tastes of your grandmother's. You get paid for the onions.|You peel half the worst sack and stir the rest of the morning. The head cook pays for the onions and teaches you a stew.}

@event gig_kitchens_burnt_pan
title: The Burnt Pan
plan: work
when: has(gig_kitchens)
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 30
art: meal
tags: job money
scene: A smoking pan on a range, a student holding it at arm's length, a cook turning round with a ladle raised.
text: You turn away for a moment and the porridge for the stay-behinds catches. The smell is already halfway to the dining hall. The head cook hasn't noticed. Yet.
> left: Own up and start again
  gold +medium
  mood -small
  alignment +tiny
  say: {~The head cook sighs the sigh of thirty years in kitchens and docks you for the oats. You make it again, properly.|"Everyone burns one," says the head cook. "Once." You are paid, minus the oats.}
> right: Scrape it and serve it
  gold +large
  alignment -tiny
  chance likely:
    say: {~Nobody notices. The stay-behinds eat it and ask for more. You don't feel great about it.|A little smoky. "Rustic," says someone. You get the full day's pay.}
  else:
    mood -medium
    say: {~A professor sends it back with a note. The head cook reads it aloud, slowly, to the whole kitchen.|The smell gives you away. You're paid in full and in disapproval.}

@event gig_kitchens_feast_night
title: Feast Night
plan: work
when: has(gig_kitchens)
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 30
art: cup
tags: job money social
scene: A steaming kitchen on a feast night, trays going out in a stream, a student in an apron grinning through the chaos.
text: {season == "winter": Tonight the castle keeps a small Midwinter table for those who stayed, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.|Tonight the stay-behinds hold a summer supper in the courtyard, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.} At breakfast the head cook chalks up the rota: somebody carries trays, somebody plates.
> left: Volunteer to carry trays
  gold +large
  health -small
  say: {~You carry until your arms forget being arms. The head cook pays you double for the night, in coins and leftovers.|Up and down the stairs until midnight. Somebody toasts the kitchens and you hear it through the door.}
> right: Ask to plate in the back
  gold +medium
  health -tiny
  say: {~You plate by the ovens and listen to the feast through the wall. Smaller pay, a scorched wrist, and the best seat in the house.|Quiet hands in a hot room. The head cook gives you the last slice of pie, which is its own wage.}

@event gig_grounds_goat
title: Bartholomew Escapes
plan: work
when: has(gig_grounds)
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 26
art: paw
tags: job nature money
scene: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a garden shed, a student with a rope below, an old woman laughing behind them.
text: {Bartholomew the goat has got out again and is standing on the shed roof, eating the thatch.|Bartholomew is on the shed roof. He has made this a habit.|The goat is on the roof. You don't even ask how any more.} Maud is laughing too hard to help. {The thatch is going fast.|He has a cabbage in his mouth.|He looks at you like a duke.}
> left: Climb up after him
  gold +large
  health -small
  vigor +tiny
  say: {You get him down after an hour and a fall. Maud pays you double for the entertainment, she says, and a little for the bruises.|Up the ladder, along the ridge, one hand on his horn. He comes down when he's ready, which is later than you are.|You climb up and bow to him. He considers it, then lets himself be led down like a duke leaving a ball. Maud pays you for the bow.}
> right: Bribe him down with an apple
  gold +medium
  mood +tiny
  say: {One apple, held very still. He considers it for a long time, like a merchant, and comes down. Maud pays for the hour you saved.|He takes the apple and stays where he is. Then, at his own pace, he comes down. Maud pays you for patience.|He takes the apple like tribute and descends. Maud pays you, and says he'll expect two next time.}

@event gig_kitchens_staff_supper
title: The Staff Supper
plan: work
when: has(gig_kitchens)
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 26
art: meal
tags: job money
scene: Cooks and kitchen hands crowded round a scrubbed table late at night, eating the day's leftovers, one student serving.
text: {At the morning briefing the head cook announces a staff supper tonight, after the last service. The whole kitchen eats at the scrubbed table, on leftovers, bread and the head cook's opinions.|Staff supper again tonight. They're saving you a place now.|The head cook bangs a ladle at breakfast. "Staff supper tonight. Sit, or serve."} {Somebody is already promising a story about a swan.|The pot-boy is asleep on his feet.|There's a cake cooling that nobody will admit to making.}
> left: Serve, then wash up
  gold +large
  health -small
  say: {You finish the washing-up at midnight. The head cook pays you extra and calls you "the useful one".|You serve, wash up, and wake the pot-boy at midnight to send him to bed. The head cook pays you for both jobs.|You serve forty bowls, wash forty bowls, and are paid for the extra hour in coins and a slice of the mystery cake.}
> right: Sit and eat with them
  gold +medium
  mood +small
  say: {You eat, and laugh at the swan story, and leave earlier with a smaller purse and a full stomach.|You sit. The cooks tell you which professors send back their soup. It's worth more than the wages.|You sit. Nobody admits to the cake, so everyone eats it. You leave full and a little poorer.}

# ---------------- The holiday versions of term jobs (X15), at their top rank ----------------

@event gig_library_stocktake
title: The Stocktake
plan: work
when: has(gig_library)
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 30
art: book
tags: job study money
scene: A deserted library in the holidays, a student on a tall ladder with a ledger, books stacked on every table, dust in the lamplight.
text: {The library in the holidays is all yours and all to be counted.|The stocktake ledger is half full; the shelves are not half counted.|You've stopped counting books and started counting floors.} {A shelf of atlases has migrated to the poetry section.|One book is not on any list and seems pleased about it.|The lamp in the east wing keeps going out, politely.}
> left: Count the restricted shelves too
  gold +large
  lore +small
  health -small
  say: {The restricted shelves count back. You finish with the right number and a bite on the thumb. The librarian dresses the bite and writes it in the ledger, under "damages".|You count every chained volume. One of them counts you. The pay is better than term-time's by a good margin.|You count the restricted floor by the lamp that keeps going out. The books count you in the dark. The pay is the best of the break.}
> right: Keep to the open stacks
  gold +large
  mood -tiny
  say: {Open stacks only, every book counted, the ledger perfect. The librarian pays you in full and says nothing about the shelves you left.|A long, dusty, honest day. You go to bed counting.|You count the open floors and leave the east wing to its lamp. The librarian pays in full and asks no questions.}

@event gig_library_lost_shelf
title: The Shelf That Wasn't There
plan: work
when: has(gig_library)
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 4
weight: 24
art: book
tags: job mystery money
scene: A narrow library aisle that seems to go on further than the building, a student holding a lamp up to an unfamiliar shelf.
text: {The stocktake finds a shelf that isn't in any ledger.|The extra shelf again. It has grown a book since yesterday.|The shelf is longer than the room it's in, now.} {The books on it have no titles.|One is warm.|One is labelled with the date of tomorrow.}
> left: Catalogue it
  gold +large
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You list every book. The librarian reads your list, goes pale and pays you to forget it. You don't.|You catalogue the shelf by lamplight. In the morning, it's gone, and your list is the only proof.|You catalogue the book labelled with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, the label has changed to the day after. The librarian pays you and takes your list.}
> right: Leave it off the ledger
  gold +medium
  guile +tiny
  say: {You don't write it down. Some shelves don't want to be counted. The librarian, passing, nods as if you'd passed a test.|You skip it. The ledger balances. The shelf, you'd swear, looks relieved.|You don't write down the book with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, it isn't there. The librarian thanks you, oddly sincerely.}

@event gig_library_last_day
title: The Ledger Balances
plan: work
when: has(gig_library)
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 3
weight: 20
art: scroll
tags: job money
scene: A head librarian signing a thick stocktake ledger at a desk under a window, a tired student waiting with ink on every finger.
text: {The last page of the stocktake. The librarian reads it slowly, all of it.|Another stocktake done, another ledger signed.|The librarian signs, and blots, and signs again for luck.} {"Not one missing," she says. "That has never happened."|"Three missing, and one extra. Normal."|She closes the ledger with both hands.}
> left: Ask for a reference
  gold +large
  renown +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {She writes one on the spot, three lines, and the third makes you blush. The pay is good and the reference is better.|She writes you a reference in a hand like engraving. It's worth more than the wages, someone tells you later.|She signs the ledger and then, without being asked, a reference. "You'll want it," she says. You will.}
> right: Ask for the afternoon off
  gold +medium
  mood +small
  say: {She gives you the afternoon and a little less pay. You spend it asleep in the reading room, which is the best use of it.|An afternoon off, and a library to yourself. You read for pleasure for the first time in months.|She closes the ledger and gives you the afternoon before you ask. You spend it in the window seat, doing nothing whatsoever.}

@event gig_newt_rush
title: The Holiday Rush
plan: work
when: has(gig_newt)
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 30
art: cup
tags: job money
scene: A packed village tavern at the holidays, garlands over the bar, every table full, a student manager behind the bar with three jugs in hand.
text: {The landlady hands you the keys over breakfast. Tonight, she warns, the Newt will be full to the rafters with farmers, barge crews and families home for the season.|Another holiday night ahead, and the Newt is already promised to a full house.|By morning, half the valley has said it's coming to the Newt tonight.} {A wedding party has sent word.|The fiddler has promised a song with forty verses.|The firewood is stacked to the ceiling.}
> left: Keep the bar open till the last song
  gold +large
  charm +small
  health -small
  say: {You pour till the song ends, then pour for the encore. The till is the heaviest you've ever counted, and your feet will never forgive you.|Closing at three, the till heavy, the floor sticky. The landlady's note says "Well done" in two places.|Half the valley comes, and you keep pouring until the firewood runs low. The till is heavy, and so is your sleep.}
> right: Close at the proper hour
  gold +large
  mood -tiny
  say: {You call time at midnight, firmly and politely. Some grumble. The till is lighter, and you sleep.|Last orders on the stroke. The till loses some takings; you keep your temper.|You close on the stroke, half the valley or not. They go home singing, which counts as a good night.}

@event gig_newt_brawl
title: A Fight Over a Song
plan: work
when: has(gig_newt)
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 24
art: cup
tags: job danger money
scene: Two barge crews squaring up in the middle of a crowded tavern, a student manager stepping between them with a tray.
text: {Two barge crews disagree about the last verse of a song, loudly.|The barge crews again, and the song again, and the same verse.|This time it's a farmer and a fisherman, and the song is a different one.} {A chair is already in the air.|Somebody has taken their coat off.|The fiddler has stopped playing.}
> left: Step between them
  challenge feat apprentice+:
    win:
      gold +large
      renown +tiny
      say: {You stop the first punch with a tray and the second with a look. Both crews buy a round to make up. You take the tips.|You walk between them and stay there. Eventually somebody laughs, and the song goes on.|The fiddler stops; you start the next verse yourself, badly. Both sides correct you, together, and forget to fight.}
    lose:
      gold +medium
      health -medium
      say: {The tray takes one punch and your jaw takes the other. They pay for the chair, at least.|You get a black eye and a free drink, which you can't drink, being on duty.|The fiddler stops, and so does the fight, just as you step in. Everyone laughs at your timing. You take the bruise for nothing.}
> right: Bribe them with a free round
  gold +medium
  charm +tiny
  say: {One round on the house, and they're singing together by the second verse. The house is a little poorer.|A free round fixes most things. The landlady's ledger will notice.|A free round, and the fiddler starts again. The farmer and the fisherman sing a duet, and the landlady's ledger winces.}

@event gig_newt_fire
title: The Fire Must Not Go Out
plan: work
when: has(gig_newt)
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 3
weight: 20
art: candle
tags: job money
scene: The great hearth of a village tavern late at night, embers banked low, a student kneeling with tongs and a bucket of ash.
text: {The landlady's fire has never gone out in her lifetime or her mother's. She's away tonight, and this morning the wind is already in the chimney.|The landlady is away again tonight, and the fire is already low. The wind is from the north.|The fire flickered last night as you locked up, as if to test you. Tonight it's yours again.} {The wood is damp.|There's frost on the inside of the windows.|The cat is sitting on the warmest stone, unhelpfully.}
> left: Sit up with it all night
  gold +large
  health -small
  say: {You feed it one stick at a time until dawn. When the landlady comes back, she puts a hand on the stones and nods at you without a word.|You sleep in the chair with the tongs in your lap. The fire lives. So, nearly, do you.|You sit up with it, cat on the warmest stone. At dawn it's still burning, and the cat has not moved once.}
> right: Bank it and trust it
  gold +large
  mood -small
  say: {You bank it the way she showed you and go to bed. You dream of ash. In the morning, it's alive, and you're shaking.|You trust it. It holds. You don't sleep a wink anyway.|You bank it and move the cat. The cat moves back. In the morning the fire's alive, and the cat is very warm.}

@event gig_pell_remedy_rush
title: Cough Season
plan: work
when: has(gig_pell)
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 30
art: bottle
tags: job alchemy money
scene: A crowded village apothecary in winter, a queue of coughing villagers, a student and an old man filling bottles as fast as they can.
text: {The whole valley is coughing, and Pell's queue is out of the door.|Day three of the cough. Pell is humming. You are not.|The queue at Pell's has its own gossip by now.} {A carter wants the cherry one.|A farmer wants something for his cow.|A professor in disguise wants something "for a friend".}
> left: Mix to order, carefully
  gold +large
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: {Every bottle mixed for the person in front of you. It's slow, and Pell pays you for every one of them.|You learn sixty coughs by ear. Pell says you're wasted on students.|You mix the disguised professor's remedy "for a friend" with great discretion. Pell charges double for the discretion and gives you half.}
> right: Mix in big batches
  gold +large
  mood -tiny
  say: {One cauldron, one recipe, three hundred bottles. It works for nearly everyone. Pell pays you and looks at the cauldron for a long time.|Fast and efficient. A farmer comes back the next day about the cow.|The professor in disguise takes a bottle from the batch and looks offended to be treated like everyone else.}

@event gig_pell_night_knock
title: A Knock After Closing
plan: work
when: has(gig_pell)
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 24
art: bottle
tags: job alchemy money
scene: An apothecary's door at night, a lantern held up to a frightened villager in the doorway, a student in a nightcap behind the counter.
text: {Pell asks you over breakfast to sleep above the shop tonight. There's a fever about, and the knocks come after closing.|Another night above the shop, Pell asks. The knocks keep coming after dark.|You're sleeping above the shop again tonight. You've stopped bothering to unlace your boots.} {Pell sleeps like a stone.|The fire goes out at ten.|The ledger says not to open after dark.}
> left: Mix any remedy yourself
  challenge brew adept-:
    win:
      gold +large
      alchemy +small
      say: {The knock comes at midnight. A shepherd, his wife burning with fever. You mix the remedy by candlelight from memory. She's better by morning; he pays in eggs and coins, and Pell pays you for the night.|The remedy is right. Pell, in the morning, checks your work and says nothing, which from him is a medal.|You sleep in your boots and mix the remedy before you're properly awake. It's right. Pell finds the mortar warm in the morning, and nods.}
    lose:
      gold +medium
      mood -small
      say: {The first batch is wrong. You wake Pell, who fixes it in a minute and pays you anyway, for trying.|You get it right on the third try, at dawn, and you shake all the next day.|You mix it half asleep and half right. The knock comes back an hour later, and Pell comes down.}
> right: Wake Pell for anything
  gold +medium
  say: {When the knock comes, Pell comes down grumbling, mixes it in two minutes and goes back to bed. You learn more watching than mixing.|You wake him. He's not cross. "That's what I'm for," he says.|You wake Pell, who's already pulling his boots on. He's started sleeping in them too.}

@event gig_pell_ledger
title: Pell's Books
plan: work
when: has(gig_pell)
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 3
weight: 20
art: scroll
tags: job money
scene: A cluttered apothecary back room, an old man and a student bent over a ledger full of crossings-out, jars all around them.
text: {Pell's account books have not been balanced since his daughters left home.|The account books again. Pell has found another drawer of receipts.|You're nearly at the bottom of the receipts. Pell is nearly at the bottom of his patience.} {Half the debts are owed to him.|Half the debts are owed by him.|Some debts are in a currency that no longer exists.}
> left: Balance them properly
  gold +large
  lore +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {Three days of receipts. At the end, Pell is owed more than he thought. He pays you out of it, generously.|You balance every page. Pell signs it as if it were a treaty.|You convert the vanished currency at an old rate you find in a drawer. Pell is owed a goat, a few crowns and an apology. He pays you from the crowns.}
> right: Forgive the small debts
  gold +medium
  alignment +tiny
  say: {You strike out every small debt owed to Pell, everything under a crown. Pell reads the list and nods. "They'd never have paid," he says. "Now they'll come back."|Small debts forgiven, the book is simpler and the village is kinder to Pell. The pay is a little less.|You forgive the debts in the vanished currency, since nobody could pay them anyway. Pell laughs for the first time in a week.}

@event gig_post_storm_run
title: The Storm Run
plan: work
when: has(gig_post)
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 30
art: feather
tags: job danger money
scene: A courier on a broom flying low over snowy fields in a storm, a sack of parcels strapped behind them, a farmhouse light ahead.
text: {The holiday post doesn't wait for weather. Tonight's route crosses the ridge in a storm.|The ridge again, and the wind again.|You've flown this route so often the farm dogs know your broom.} {The sack is heavier than the broom likes.|One parcel is labelled "fragile" in four languages.|The light at the last farm is always on for you.}
> left: Fly straight over the ridge
  challenge feat adept wild:
    win:
      gold +large
      vigor +small
      say: {Over the ridge and down to the farms in half the time. The bonus for early delivery is very good.|The wind does its worst and you do better. Every parcel delivered, dry.|You fly the ridge with the farm dogs barking you home. The light at the last farm is on, and the bonus is too.}
    lose:
      gold +medium
      health -medium
      say: {The wind takes you into a hedge. The parcels survive. You are less sure about yourself.|You deliver everything, late and frozen, with a sprained wrist.|The storm hides the light at the last farm. You find it by the dogs, an hour late and very wet.}
> right: Take the valley road
  gold +large
  mood -tiny
  say: {The long way round, low and slow. Late, but every parcel's in one piece, and so are you.|The valley road adds two hours and no danger. The pay is the pay.|You take the valley road. The light at the last farm is still on when you get there, late. Somebody waited.}

@event gig_post_wrong_parcel
title: The Wrong Parcel
plan: work
when: has(gig_post)
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 24
art: letter
tags: job money
scene: A farmhouse doorway in the snow, an old woman holding up a parcel that is clearly ticking, a courier on the step looking worried.
text: {A parcel you delivered yesterday has come back. The old woman says it isn't hers, and it's ticking.|Another wrong address. The holiday post is chaos.|The labels are getting worse. One just says "Aunt".} {The ticking is getting faster.|It smells of oranges.|It's addressed to someone who died years ago.}
> left: Find the right house tonight
  gold +large
  health -tiny
  say: {You fly the valley till midnight and find the right house. The ticking was a clock, a present for a grandfather. He gives you a slice of cake and a coin.|You find the owner three farms over. The bonus for "lost and found" is surprisingly good.|The person it's addressed to died years ago. You find their granddaughter instead, who opens it in the doorway and laughs and cries at once. The bonus is good.}
> right: Return it to the loft
  gold +medium
  mood -tiny
  say: {You take it back to the loft. Someone else will fly it tomorrow, and get the bonus.|The loft-master sighs and puts it on the shelf of the lost. It ticks there all week.|You take it back. The loft-master reads the name, goes quiet, and says he'll deliver this one himself.}

@event gig_post_last_parcel
title: The Last Parcel
plan: work
when: has(gig_post)
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 3
weight: 20
art: letter
tags: job money
scene: An empty courier loft at the end of a holiday shift, one small parcel left on the shelf, addressed in shaky handwriting.
text: {The last parcel of the day is small, and addressed in a shaky hand to a cottage on the far moor.|One parcel left again. Always one.|The last parcel, and the last daylight.} {It's light as a feather.|It's warm.|It rattles like a box of buttons.}
> left: Fly it out now
  gold +large
  health -small
  say: {The cottage is an hour into the dark. An old man opens the door and holds the parcel as if it were alive. "From my son," he says. The pay feels like the least of it.|You deliver it at dusk to a woman who cries. You don't ask why. You fly home slowly.|It rattles all the way across the moor. Buttons, the woman says at the door, from her mother's coat. She gives you one.}
> right: Leave it for the morning
  gold +large
  mood -small
  say: {It waits on the shelf all night and you think about it all night. You fly it at dawn.|In the morning you deliver it, a day late. Nobody minds but you.|You leave it till morning, last light being gone. It rattles on the shelf all night, as if impatient.}
